      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Long’s promotional blitz featured stars like Ronald Reagan and Buster Keaton, who appeared in short films to showcase model homes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        10,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maryvale%2C_Phoenix",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Maryvale was one of the country’s first master‑planned suburbs, built with an assembly‑line speed that let Long finish eight homes a day.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maryvale%2C_Phoenix",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1954, Long had sold 350 homes before construction even began, a record for a new subdivision at the time.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maryvale%2C_Phoenix",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lucky’s, opened on Grand Avenue in 1966, started as a casino concept but became a lively music venue known as the “Home of the Stars.”.",
